course: dictionary
lesson: 10680
title: Unpaid
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unpaid means money has not been handed over yet, or work was done for no money at all. Look at the words around it to find which meaning fits. «An unpaid loaf of bread is a loss to the shop» tells you the bread was taken without money being paid for it.
explain: The word is made of un- and paid. Un- often means not, so unpaid means not paid. When you meet an unknown word, try swapping in a simpler meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Look at «Many grown-ups become unpaid carers for their own parents, helping with medicine and meals.» The clues are helping and meals, which are things done for family. These carers get no money for it, so unpaid means done for no money. Swap in "done for no money" and the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Unpaid means not paid yet, or done without getting money.
fact: «Unpaid bills» are bills that are still owed.
fact: «Unpaid work» is work done for no money, like volunteers helping others.
fact: «Unpaid leave» means time off work without pay.
fact: Un- at the start of a word often means not, so unpaid means not paid.
exercise: «An unpaid loaf of bread is a loss to the shop.» What does unpaid mean here? | Baked by the shop itself | Given away free by the shop | Paid for twice | *Not paid for yet
exercise: Volunteers help at the school fair and get no money. What kind of work is this? | Paid work | Extra work | Holiday work | *Unpaid work
exercise: Which word from the sentence «Scam texts about unpaid bills trick many people» means money still owed? | Scam | *Unpaid | Texts | People
test: «Mum took unpaid leave to look after me when I was poorly.» What does unpaid leave mean? | Time spent at the bank | *Time off work without pay | Working extra hours for free | Time off with full pay
test: Which sentence uses unpaid to mean done without getting money? | «The shop had a sale on bread.» | *«Many grown-ups become unpaid carers for their parents.» | «The bus was late this morning.» | «The unpaid bill was in the post.»
exam: Ella reads «Some debt can trap people, like an unpaid credit card.» What does unpaid mean here, and how do you know? | *Not paid yet, because the debt is still owed | Free to use, because it has no cost | Lost, because the card was thrown away | Paid off in full, because the card is closed
